abstract: >-
  Regularized Adjusted Plus-Minus computed from public play-by-play for NBA
  2016-17 through 2024-25, validated at a Spearman correlation of 0.914 against
  an independently constructed stint dataset. Shipped alongside it are the
  measured split-half reliability of fifteen box-score metrics across
  twenty-five seasons, and the possession coefficient measured per season rather
  than assumed at the conventional 0.44, which came out below that convention in
  every season measured.

  The project set out to fuse public metrics by weighting each by its
  reliability, and the measurements ruled that out. Across this metric set
  reliability correlates -0.564 with correlation against RAPM, so the most
  repeatable metrics are the least related to winning. Reliability is therefore
  used as a bound on how much of a metric a measurement-error model may
  attribute to the latent quantity, never as a weight. The resulting fused
  rating does not predict next-season team net rating better than RAPM alone
  and is not statistically distinguishable from it, a negative result the
  project publishes rather than omits.
